[14.054, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u25cf triage_cluster\u001b[0m\u001b[2m({})\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[14.189, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m 5 nodes, 3 healthy\r\n"]
[14.189, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m \u001b[31mCRITICAL\u001b[0m \u001b[2mkubernetes\u001b[0m k8s-w1    \u001b[1mescalate_hardware\u001b[0m  The kernel remounted a filesystem read-only, which\u2026\r\n"]
[14.189, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m \u001b[31mCRITICAL\u001b[0m \u001b[2mslurm     \u001b[0m slurm-c1  \u001b[1mescalate_hardware\u001b[0m  Hardware fault: the node registered with less\u2026\r\n"]
[16.323, "o", "  \u001b[36m\u25cf node_detail\u001b[0m\u001b[2m({\"node\": \"k8s-w1\"})\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[16.422, "o", "    \u001b[2m\u2514\u001b[0m {\"conditions\": [\"KernelDeadlock=False (KernelHasNoDeadlock) since 2026-09-26T21:49:46Z\", \"Readonly\u2026\r\n"]
[17.922, "o", "\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  Two nodes need attention out of 5 (the other 3 are healthy). Both are **critical, `escalate_hardware`**.\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  ## 1. k8s-w1 (Kubernetes): read-only root filesystem, still schedulable\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  **Summary from triage:** \"The kernel remounted a filesystem read-only, which usually means a disk or\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  filesystem error. Take the node out.\"\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  **Evidence:**\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  - `Ready=True`, but `ReadonlyFilesystem=True (FilesystemIsReadOnly)`: `EXT4-fs (vda1): Remounting\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  filesystem read-only`\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  - The fault was first reported at 21:44:17Z. **Someone cordoned the node at 21:48:16Z and uncordoned it at\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  21:49:58Z.** node-problem-detector reported the fault again at 22:02:45Z, so the node is taking new pods\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  right now on a read-only disk.\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  - 11 workload pods are still on it, including **both CoreDNS replicas** (`coredns-...-vzd84`,\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  `coredns-...-wbkkg`), argocd-application-controller-0, argocd-server, cert-manager-cainjector,\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  alertmanager, grafana, kube-state-metrics, hubble-relay/ui and `slurm/slurm-worker-slinky-0`.\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  **Next steps (from triage):**\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  1. Cordon, then drain. Pods writing to local disk are already failing.\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  2. Check the kernel log for I/O errors and the disk's SMART data before any reboot.\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  3. Open a hardware ticket if the disk is failing. Only fsck and reboot if it isn't.\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  **Suggested commands:**\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  ```\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  kubectl cordon k8s-w1\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  kubectl drain k8s-w1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=5m\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  ssh k8s-w1 \"dmesg -T | grep -iE 'I/O error|remount|EXT4-fs error'\"\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  ```\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  Both CoreDNS replicas are on this node, so check that they can be rescheduled onto other nodes before you\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  drain it. Otherwise cluster DNS goes down during the eviction. Also find out who uncordoned it at 21:49\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  and why.\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  ## 2. slurm-c1 (Slurm): missing GPU, chronic node\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  **Summary from triage:** \"Hardware fault: the node registered with less hardware than configured. Keep it\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  out of service.\"\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  **Evidence:**\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  - State `IDLE+DRAIN+INVALID_REG`, CPUs 0/2 allocated, GRES `gpu:fake:3`\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  - Reason `\"gres/gpu count reported lower than configured (3 < 4)\"`, set 10s ago by `slurm`\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  - slurmd registered 3 of the 4 configured GPUs\r\n"]
[17.923, "o", "  - **7 drain/down events** for this node in the event window (the chronic threshold is 3)\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  **Next steps (from triage):**\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  1. Keep the node drained. Don't resume it until the hardware is fixed.\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  2. Open a hardware ticket with the reason text and node serial. On real GPUs, pull `nvidia-smi -q` and the\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  dmesg Xid lines first.\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  3. After the repair, run a burn-in job in the `burnin` partition before returning it to batch.\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  **Suggested commands:**\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  ```\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  scontrol show node slurm-c1\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  ssh slurm-c1 'journalctl -u slurmd -n 50; dmesg | tail -50'\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  sbatch -p burnin -w slurm-c1 --gres=gpu:4 burnin.sh   # after repair\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  ```\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  slurm-c1 is already drained and idle, so nothing needs to be done in the scheduler. It just needs the\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  ticket.\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  ---\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  **Should I cordon k8s-w1 now?** That stops new pods from landing on it but leaves the running pods in\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  place. I haven't changed anything yet. Evicting the pods with `kubectl drain` is something you'd run\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  yourself.\r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  \r\n"]
[17.924, "o", "  This triage only covers what the schedulers report. It doesn't read hardware sensors or GPU telemetry.\r\n"]
[29.924, "o", "\r\n"]
[29.924, "o", "\u001b[1m3. Take the Kubernetes node out\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[29.925, "o", "\r\n"]
[29.925, "o", "\u001b[35m\u001b[1myou \u203a\u001b[0m "]
[29.925, "o", "T"]
